    <exercise xml:id="ez-8-3-WW3">
      <title>Convergence of a series and its sequence</title>

      <webwork source="Library/maCalcDB/setSeries4Geometric/ns8_2_9.pg" />
    </exercise>
    <!-- Uses EV2 and incompatible with PTX --> <!--
            <exercise xml:id="ez-8-3-WW4">
              <webwork source="Library/Rochester/setSeries5IntegralTest/ur_sr_5_11.pg" />
              </exercise>
              -->
    <exercise xml:id="ez-8-3-WW5">
      <title>Convergence of an integral and a related series</title>

      <webwork source="Library/Rochester/setSeries5IntegralTest/ur_sr_5_12.pg" />
    </exercise>
    <!-- Uses EV2 and incompatible with PTX --> <!--
                <exercise xml:id="ez-8-3-WW6">
                  <webwork source="Library/Rochester/setSeries6CompTests/benny_ser3.pg" />
                  </exercise>
                  --> <!--
    Uses EV2 and incompatible with PTX --> <!--
                  <exercise xml:id="ez-8-3-WW7">
                    <webwork source="Library/Rochester/setSeries6CompTests/benny_ser4.pg" />
                    </exercise>
                    -->
    <exercise xml:id="adding-series">
    	<title> Adding two series together </title>
    	<introduction>
    		<p> The associative and commutative laws of addition allow us to add finite
    		sums in any order we want. That is, if <m>\sum_{k=0}^n a_k</m> and <m>\sum_{k=0}^n b_k</m>
    		are finite sums of real numbers, then <md>
    		  \sum_{k=0}^{n} a_k + \sum_{k=0}^n b_k = \sum_{k=0}^n (a_k+b_k)
    		</md>
    		. </p>
    		<p>
    			However, we do need to be careful extending rules like this to infinite
    			series.
    		</p>
    	</introduction>

    	<task>
    		<statement>
    			<p> Let <m>a_n = 1 + \frac{1}{2^n}</m> and <m>b_n = -1</m> for each
    			nonnegative integer <m>n</m>. <ol marker="i.">
    			        <li>
    			          <p> Explain why the series <m>\sum_{k=0}^{\infty} a_k</m> and <m>\sum_{k=0}^{\infty}
    			b_k</m> both diverge. </p>
    			        </li>

    			        <li>
    			          <p> Explain why the series <m>\sum_{k=0}^{\infty} (a_k+b_k)</m>
    			converges. </p>
    			        </li>

    			        <li>
    			          <p> Explain why <md>
    			              \sum_{k=0}^{\infty} a_k + \sum_{k=0}^{\infty} b_k \neq \sum_{k=0}^{\infty}
    			(a_k+b_k)
    			            </md>.
    			This shows that it is possible to have to two divergent series <m>\sum_{k=0}^{\infty} a_k</m>
    			and <m>\sum_{k=0}^{\infty} b_k</m> but yet have the series <m>\sum_{k=0}^{\infty}
    			              (a_k+b_k)</m> converge. </p>
    			        </li>
    			      </ol>
    			    </p>
    		</statement>
    		<answer>
    			<p>
    			  <ol marker="i.">
    			    <li>
    			      <p>
    			        <m>a_n = 1 + \frac{1}{2^n} \to 1 \ne 0</m> and <m>b_n = -1 \to -1 \ne 0</m>. </p>
    			    </li>

    			    <li>
    			      <p> The series is geometric with <m>r = \frac{1}{2}</m>. </p>
    			    </li>

    			    <li>
    			      <p>
    			        Since the two individual series diverge, neither sum is a finite number, so it
    			        doesn't make any sense to add them.
    			      </p>
    			    </li>
    			  </ol>
    			</p>
    		</answer>
    		<solution>
    			<p>
    			  <ol marker="i.">
    			    <li>
    			      <p> Since <m>a_n = 1 + \frac{1}{2^n} \to 1 \ne 0</m> and <m>b_n =
    			          -1 \to -1 \ne 0</m> as <m>n \to \infty</m>, by the Divergence Test both <m>\sum_{k=0}^{\infty}
    			        a_k</m> and <m>\sum_{k=0}^{\infty} b_k</m> diverge. </p>
    			    </li>

    			    <li>
    			      <p> However, the series <md>
    			          \sum_{k=0}^{\infty} (a_k+b_k) = \sum_{k=0}^{\infty} (1 + \frac{1}{2^k} - 1)
    			          = \sum_{k=0}^{\infty} \frac{1}{2^k}
    			        </md>
    			        converges because the series is geometric with <m>r = \frac{1}{2}</m>. </p>
    			    </li>

    			    <li>
    			      <p> The issue is that it doesn't make any sense to talk about <md>
    			          \sum_{k=0}^{\infty} a_k + \sum_{k=0}^{\infty} b_k
    			        </md>.
    			        when <m>\sum_{k=0}^{\infty} a_k</m> and <m>\sum_{k=0}^{\infty} b_k</m>
    			        diverge. Since those series diverge, neither sum is a finite number, so it
    			        doesn't make any sense to add them. </p>
    			    </li>
    			  </ol>
    			</p>
    		</solution>
    	</task>

    	<task>
    		<statement>
    			<p> While part (a) shows that we cannot add series term by term in
    			general, we can under reasonable conditions. The problem in part (a) is that we tried to
    			add divergent series. In this exercise we will show that if <m>\sum a_k</m> and <m>\sum
    			        b_k</m> are convergent series, then <m>\sum (a_k+b_k)</m> is a convergent series
    			and <md>
    			        \sum (a_k+b_k) = \sum a_k + \sum b_k
    			      </md> . <ol marker="i.">
    			        <li>
    			          <p> Let <m>A_n</m> and <m>B_n</m> be the <m>n</m>th partial sums of
    			the series <m>\sum_{k=1}^{\infty} a_k</m> and <m>\sum_{k=1}^{\infty} b_k</m>,
    			respectively. Explain why <md>
    			              A_n + B_n = \sum_{k=1}^n (a_k+b_k)
    			            </md>
    			. </p>
    			        </li>

    			        <li>
    			          <p> Use the previous result and properties of limits to show that <md>
    			              \sum_{k=1}^{\infty} (a_k+b_k) = \sum_{k=1}^{\infty} a_k +
    			              \sum_{k=1}^{\infty} b_k
    			            </md>.
    			(Note that the starting point of the sum is irrelevant in this problem, so it doesn't
    			matter where we begin the sum.) </p>
    			        </li>
    			      </ol>
    			    </p>
    		</statement>
    		<answer>
    			<p>
    			  <ol marker="i.">
    			    <li>
    			      <p> Note that <m> A_n + B_n = (a_1 + b_1) + (a_2 + b_2) + \cdots +
    			        (a_n + b_n) </m>. </p>
    			    </li>

    			    <li>
    			      <p> Note that <m> \lim_{n \to \infty} \sum_{k=1}^n (a_k+b_k) =
    			        \lim_{n \to \infty} \left( \sum_{k=1}^{n} a_k + \sum_{k=1}^{n} b_k \right) </m>
    			        . </p>
    			    </li>
    			  </ol>
    			</p>
    		</answer>
    		<solution>
    			<p>
    			  <ol marker="i.">
    			    <li>
    			      <p> Let <m>A_n</m> and <m>B_n</m> be the <m>n</m>th partial sums of
    			        the series <m>\sum_{k=1}^{\infty} a_k</m> and <m>\sum_{k=1}^{\infty} b_k</m>,
    			        respectively. By definition, <md>
    			          A_n + B_n = \sum_{k=1}^{n} a_k + \sum_{k=1}^{n} b_k
    			        </md>.
    			        Expanding these finite sums, we have <md>
    			          A_n + B_n = (a_1 + a_2 + \cdots a_n) + (b_1 + b_2 + cdots + b_n)
    			        </md>.
    			        By the associative and commutative properties of finite sums, we can rearrange
    			        these sums in any order we wish, and also group the terms in any pairings we
    			        wish. Thus, <md>
    			          A_n + B_n = (a_1 + b_1) + (a_2 + b_2) + \cdots + (a_n + b_n)
    			        </md>,
    			        and therefore <md>
    			          A_n + B_n = \sum_{k=1}^n (a_k+b_k)
    			        </md>
    			        . </p>
    			    </li>

    			    <li>
    			      <p> The last equality we demonstrated above tells us that <md>
    			          \sum_{k=1}^n (a_k+b_k) = \sum_{k=1}^{n} a_k + \sum_{k=1}^{n} b_k
    			        </md>.
    			        In this equality, we can take the limit of both sides as <m>n \to \infty</m>.
    			        On the right side, we know that both <m>\lim_{n \to \infty} \sum_{k=1}^{n} a_k</m>
    			        and <m>\lim_{n \to \infty} \sum_{k=1}^{n} b_k</m> exist because the two series
    			        converge, and therefore the limit of the sum is the sum of the limits. Hence, <md>
    			          <mrow>\lim_{n \to \infty} \sum_{k=1}^n (a_k+b_k) &amp;= \lim_{n \to \infty}
    			        \left( \sum_{k=1}^{n} a_k + \sum_{k=1}^{n} b_k \right)</mrow>
    			          <mrow>&amp;= \lim_{n \to \infty} \sum_{k=1}^{n} a_k + \lim_{n \to \infty}
    			        \sum_{k=1}^{n} b_k</mrow>
    			        </md>. Thus we have shown that <md>
    			          \sum_{k=1}^{\infty} (a_k+b_k) = \sum_{k=1}^{\infty} a_k +
    			          \sum_{k=1}^{\infty} b_k
    			        </md>
    			        . </p>
    			    </li>
    			  </ol>
    			</p>
    		</solution>
    	</task>

    	<task>
    		<statement>
    			<p> Use the prior result to find the sum of the series <m>\sum_{k=0}^{\infty}
    			\frac{2^k+3^k}{5^k}</m>. </p>
    		</statement>
    		<answer>
    			<p>
    			<m> \sum_{k=0}^{\infty} \frac{2^k+3^k}{5^k} = \frac{25}{6} </m>. </p>
    		</answer>
    		<solution>
    			<p> Since both <m>\sum_{k=0}^{\infty} \left( \frac{2}{5} \right)^k</m>
    			and <m>\sum_{k=0}^{\infty} \left( \frac{3}{5} \right)^k</m> are convergent geometric
    			series, we can apply the result from the last exercise above to say that <md>
    			  \sum_{k=0}^{\infty} \frac{2^k+3^k}{5^k} = \sum_{k=0}^{\infty} \left( \frac{2}{5}
    			\right)^k + \sum_{k=0}^{\infty} \left( \frac{3}{5} \right)^k
    			</md>.
    			Applying the known sum of geometric series to each sum on the right, we then have <md>
    			  \sum_{k=0}^{\infty} \frac{2^k+3^k}{5^k} = \frac{1}{1 - \frac{2}{5}} + \frac{1}{1 -
    			\frac{3}{5}} = \frac{5}{3} + \frac{5}{2} = \frac{25}{6}
    			</md>
    			. </p>
    		</solution>
    	</task>
    </exercise>

